###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#mapping_1
:mapping_1 rdf:type :AnalogicalMapping ;
                rdfs:label "The text 'the first rule of deaf club..' is mapped onto the scene of sign language communication."@en ;
                rdfs:comment "The top text 'the first rule of deaf club..' is juxtaposed with the visual of a woman using American Sign Language (ASL) to communicate. This creates an analogical mapping where the text refers to a rule governing the 'deaf club' (the deaf community), and the visual material (the woman signing) represents a member of that club actively engaging in its communication method. The mapping implies that the visual scene is an instance of the 'rule' being enacted or referenced."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "90826.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-19T17:13:38.848568" .

###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#semiotic_projection_1
:semiotic_projection_1 rdf:type :SemioticProjection ;
                rdfs:label "User projected onto the textual material conveying the rule of the deaf club"@en ;
                rdfs:comment "The user is projected onto the bottom text 'nyou nu not nalk amout nef-glub', which directly addresses the viewer with a deictic imperative ('nyou' meaning 'you') instructing them not to talk about the deaf club. This creates a direct address to the user, making them the subject of the rule being enforced. The top text 'the first rule of deaf club..' sets up the context, but the bottom text explicitly engages the user as 'you'."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "90826.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-19T17:13:38.848568" .

